arXiv:2602.23242v1 [cs.AI] 26 Feb 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line" lang="en-US">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">A Model-Free Universal AI</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname"><a href="mailto:&lt;yegonkim@kaist.ac.kr&gt;?Subject=Your%20paper" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Yegon Kim</a>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
Graduate School of AI
<br class="ltx_break">KAIST
<br class="ltx_break">Seoul, South Korea

</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Juho Lee
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
Graduate School of AI
<br class="ltx_break">KAIST
<br class="ltx_break">Seoul, South Korea

</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In general reinforcement learning, all established optimal agents, including AIXI, are model-based, explicitly maintaining and using environment models.
This paper introduces Universal AI with Q-Induction (AIQI), the first model-free agent proven to be asymptotically <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal in general RL.
AIQI performs universal induction over distributional action-value functions, instead of policies or environments like previous works.
Under a grain of truth condition, we prove that AIQI is strong asymptotically <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal and asymptotically <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-Bayes-optimal.
Our results significantly expand the diversity of known universal agents.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The theory of general reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lattimore, 2014</a>, GRL;]</cite> provides a framework for studying agents in the most general class of environments, those that satisfy only minimal structural assumptions.
In this setting, AIXI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hutter, 2000</a>]</cite> is a foundational theoretical model: it combines universal induction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Solomonoff, 1964a</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Solomonoff, 1964b</a>]</cite> with sequential decision theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Neumann and Morgenstern, 1944</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bellman, 1957</a>]</cite> to define a Bayes-optimal agent.
Although AIXI is uncomputable, it serves as a useful theoretical model of powerful goal-driven AIs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Orseau and Ring, 2012</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Everitt et al., 2016</a>]</cite>, and has also been used to define a universal measure of intelligence, called the Legg-Hutter intelligence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Legg and Hutter, 2007</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Interestingly, AIXI and all other established optimal agents in GRL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hutter, 2000</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lattimore and Hutter, 2014</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leike et al., 2017</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen et al., 2019</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Catt et al., 2023</a>]</cite>, otherwise known as universal agents or universal AI, are <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">model-based</em>: they explicitly infer and make use of a model of the environment (a world model).
In contrast, the dominant paradigm in practical RL is <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">model-free</em>, learning value functions or policies directly from experience <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Watkins, 1989</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Williams, 1992</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rummery and Niranjan, 1994</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Konda and Tsitsiklis, 1999</a>]</cite>.
Showing that a model-free algorithm is optimal in GRL has therefore remained elusive and has been repeatedly highlighted as an open problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Everitt and Hutter, 2018</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Catt, 2022</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hutter et al., 2024</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we present Universal <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AI</span> with <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q</span>-<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I</span>nduction (AIQI), a model-free agent that performs universal induction over <em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">return-predictors</em>, objects similar to distributional Q-values <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bellemare et al., 2017</a>]</cite>.
AIQI is essentially an <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-greedy, on-policy distributional Monte Carlo control algorithm.
Under a grain of truth condition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kalai and Lehrer, 1993</a>]</cite>,
we prove that AIQI achieves strong asymptotic <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-optimality and asymptotic <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-Bayes-optimality in GRL.
Our results significantly expand the class of known universal agents,
and provide a blueprint for the analysis of other policy iteration algorithms in general environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>General Reinforcement Learning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We establish the basic notation for general reinforcement learning.
We also provide a summary of all the important symbols in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>.
Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}\subseteq[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}\subseteq[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the finite sets of actions, observations, and rewards, respectively. The percept space is <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}:=\mathcal{O}\times\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}:=\mathcal{O}\times\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a history <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h_{1:t}\in\mathcal{H}:=(\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{E})^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1:t}\in\mathcal{H}:=(\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{E})^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of actions and percepts.
We write <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h_{1:t}=a_{1}e_{1}\dots a_{t}e_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1:t}=a_{1}e_{1}\dots a_{t}e_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for the history up to time <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{t}:=(\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{E})^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{t}:=(\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{E})^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of all histories up to time <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
A policy <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\mathcal{H}\to\Delta\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\mathcal{H}\to\Delta\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> maps histories to action distributions, while an environment <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu:\mathcal{H}\times\mathcal{A}\to\Delta\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu:\mathcal{H}\times\mathcal{A}\to\Delta\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> maps history-action pairs to percept distributions.
When policy <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> interacts with environment <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, they induce a distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu^{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu^{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\nu^{\pi}(h_{1:t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nu^{\pi}(h_{1:t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle:={\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}\pi(a_{1})}\,{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}\nu(e_{1}|a_{1})}\,\cdots\,{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}\pi(a_{t}|h_{&lt;t})}\,{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}\nu(e_{t}|h_{&lt;t}a_{t})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">π</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">a</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">ν</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">e</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" fence="false" mathcolor="#1E4678" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">a</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">1</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">⋯</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">π</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">a</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" fence="false" mathcolor="#8C3232" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">&lt;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">ν</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">e</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" fence="false" mathcolor="#1E4678" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">&lt;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">t</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">a</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">t</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:={\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}\pi(a_{1})}\,{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}\nu(e_{1}|a_{1})}\,\cdots\,{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}\pi(a_{t}|h_{&lt;t})}\,{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}\nu(e_{t}|h_{&lt;t}a_{t})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phantom{:}=\textstyle{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}\prod_{1\leq i\leq t}\pi(a_{i}\mid h_{&lt;i})}\,{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}\prod_{1\leq i\leq t}\nu(e_{i}\mid h_{&lt;i}a_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">∏</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">≤</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">≤</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">a</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" fence="true" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#8C3232" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">&lt;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">∏</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">≤</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">i</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">≤</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">e</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">i</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" fence="true" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#1E4678" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">&lt;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">a</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">i</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phantom{:}=\textstyle{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}\prod_{1\leq i\leq t}\pi(a_{i}\mid h_{&lt;i})}\,{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}\prod_{1\leq i\leq t}\nu(e_{i}\mid h_{&lt;i}a_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\phantom{:}={\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}\pi(a_{1:t}\parallel e_{&lt;t})}\,{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}\nu(e_{1:t}\parallel a_{1:t})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">π</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">a</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#8C3232" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#8C3232" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">e</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">&lt;</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">ν</mi><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">e</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#1E4678" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" lspace="0em" mathcolor="#1E4678" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">a</mi><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" lspace="0.278em" mathcolor="#1E4678" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phantom{:}={\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}\pi(a_{1:t}\parallel e_{&lt;t})}\,{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}\nu(e_{1:t}\parallel a_{1:t})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This is a valid distribution, that is, the probabilities sum to 1 across all histories <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h_{1:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1:t}</annotation></semantics></math>. A direct consequence is that for a deterministic policy <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sum_{e_{1:t}\in\mathcal{E}^{t}}\nu\left(e_{1:t}\parallel\pi^{\prime}\left(e_{&lt;t}\right)\right)=1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{e_{1:t}\in\mathcal{E}^{t}}\nu\left(e_{1:t}\parallel\pi^{\prime}\left(e_{&lt;t}\right)\right)=1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\prime}(e_{&lt;t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\prime}(e_{&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the sequence of actions <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{1:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1:t}</annotation></semantics></math> taken by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> under fixed percepts <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We also define the sample space <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega:=(\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{E})^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega:=(\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{E})^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, which consists of all <em id="S2.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">infinite</em> action-percept sequences <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. Every history <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h_{1:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1:t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a prefix of some outcome <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a discount factor <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the (full) <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">return</em> at time <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}:=(1-\gamma)\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{k}r_{t+k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}:=(1-\gamma)\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{k}r_{t+k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{1-\gamma}=\left(\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{k}\right)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{1-\gamma}=\left(\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{k}\right)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that returns lie in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, like the rewards.
This definition assumes a geometric discount, and we discuss the generalization to general discount sequences in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">value function</em> for policy <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> in environment <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi}_{\nu}(h_{&lt;t}):=\mathbb{E}^{\pi}_{\nu}\left[R_{t}\mid h_{&lt;t}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi}_{\nu}(h_{&lt;t}):=\mathbb{E}^{\pi}_{\nu}\left[R_{t}\mid h_{&lt;t}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}^{\pi}_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}^{\pi}_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> is the expectation with respect to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu^{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu^{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The optimal value is defined as <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\ast}_{\nu}=\sup_{\pi}V^{\pi}_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>π</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\ast}_{\nu}=\sup_{\pi}V^{\pi}_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math>, and an optimal policy <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\ast}_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\ast}_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> is any policy that achieves this supremum.
The action-value, or Q-value function, is <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\pi}_{\nu}(h_{&lt;t},a_{t}):=\mathbb{E}^{\pi}_{\nu}\left[R_{t}\mid h_{&lt;t}a_{t}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\pi}_{\nu}(h_{&lt;t},a_{t}):=\mathbb{E}^{\pi}_{\nu}\left[R_{t}\mid h_{&lt;t}a_{t}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Value functions satisfy the Bellman equations, which allow us to express one with the other:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{\nu}^{\pi}(h_{&lt;t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{\nu}^{\pi}(h_{&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\!=\!\mathbb{E}_{a_{t}\sim\pi(\cdot\mid h_{&lt;t})}\left[Q_{\nu}^{\pi}(h_{&lt;t},a_{t})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.108em">=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!=\!\mathbb{E}_{a_{t}\sim\pi(\cdot\mid h_{&lt;t})}\left[Q_{\nu}^{\pi}(h_{&lt;t},a_{t})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q_{\nu}^{\pi}(h_{&lt;t},a_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q_{\nu}^{\pi}(h_{&lt;t},a_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\!=\!\mathbb{E}_{e^{\prime}_{t}\sim\nu(\cdot\mid h_{&lt;t},a_{t})}\left[(1-\gamma)r_{t}+\gamma V_{\nu}^{\pi}(h^{\prime}_{&lt;t+1})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.108em">=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!=\!\mathbb{E}_{e^{\prime}_{t}\sim\nu(\cdot\mid h_{&lt;t},a_{t})}\left[(1-\gamma)r_{t}+\gamma V_{\nu}^{\pi}(h^{\prime}_{&lt;t+1})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Obtaining the full return requires simulating or experiencing an infinite length trajectory. Given some horizon length <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-step return is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t,H}:=(1-\gamma)\sum_{k=0}^{H-1}\gamma^{k}r_{t+k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t,H}:=(1-\gamma)\sum_{k=0}^{H-1}\gamma^{k}r_{t+k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">which can be computed with <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> steps of agent-environment interactions.
The <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-step value function is defined as
<math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi}_{\nu,H}(h_{&lt;t}):=\mathbb{E}^{\pi}_{\nu}\left[R_{t,H}\mid h_{&lt;t}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi}_{\nu,H}(h_{&lt;t}):=\mathbb{E}^{\pi}_{\nu}\left[R_{t,H}\mid h_{&lt;t}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, we define the effective horizon as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Effective horizon)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>-effective horizon is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\eta):=\min\left\{H\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}\;\middle|\;(1-\gamma)\sum_{k=H}^{\infty}\gamma^{k}\leq\eta\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\eta):=\min\left\{H\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}\;\middle|\;(1-\gamma)\sum_{k=H}^{\infty}\gamma^{k}\leq\eta\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">One can show that
the <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\eta)</annotation></semantics></math>-step return differs from the full return by at most <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, and likewise for the value function:
<math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}-R_{t,H(\eta)}=(1-\gamma)\sum_{k=H(\eta)}^{\infty}\gamma^{k}r_{t+k}\leq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}-R_{t,H(\eta)}=(1-\gamma)\sum_{k=H(\eta)}^{\infty}\gamma^{k}r_{t+k}\leq\eta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>AIXI</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To define AIXI, we first need to introduce the mixture environment <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Mixture environment)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a countable environment class <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and a prior distribution <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\nu)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\nu)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> over the environments <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, the mixture environment <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the unique environment that satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\xi(e_{1:t}\parallel a_{1:t})=\sum_{\nu\in\mathcal{M}}w(\nu)\,\nu(e_{1:t}\parallel a_{1:t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></munder><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi(e_{1:t}\parallel a_{1:t})=\sum_{\nu\in\mathcal{M}}w(\nu)\,\nu(e_{1:t}\parallel a_{1:t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Equivalently, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi^{\pi}(\cdot)=\sum_{\nu}w(\nu)\nu^{\pi}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>ν</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{\pi}(\cdot)=\sum_{\nu}w(\nu)\nu^{\pi}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> for any policy <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">AIXI is then defined as the optimal policy in the mixture environment <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>. By its definition, AIXI is the Bayes-optimal agent with respect to the prior <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\nu)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(AIXI)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AIXI is the optimal policy <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\ast}_{\xi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\ast}_{\xi}</annotation></semantics></math> in the mixture environment <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\ast}_{\xi}(h_{&lt;t}):=\argmax_{a}Q^{\ast}_{\xi}(h_{&lt;t},a)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\ast}_{\xi}(h_{&lt;t}):=\argmax_{a}Q^{\ast}_{\xi}(h_{&lt;t},a).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Universal AI with Q-Induction</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present Universal AI with Q-Induction (AIQI), a model-free approach to general reinforcement learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a discretization level <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\mathbb{Z}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>-discretized <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-step return at time <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}:=\frac{\left\lfloor MR_{t,H}\right\rfloor}{M},\quad z_{t}\in\mathcal{Z}:=\left\{0,\frac{1}{M},\dotsc,\frac{M-1}{M}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}:=\frac{\left\lfloor MR_{t,H}\right\rfloor}{M},\quad z_{t}\in\mathcal{Z}:=\left\{0,\frac{1}{M},\dotsc,\frac{M-1}{M}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is an approximation of <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t,H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t,H}</annotation></semantics></math> whose error gets smaller as <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, it always holds that <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t,H}-z_{t}&lt;1/M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t,H}-z_{t}&lt;1/M</annotation></semantics></math>.
We choose <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as the target of prediction instead of <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t,H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t,H}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Although this choice isn’t necessary for our case of geometric discounting, there exist general discount sequences (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) where <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t,H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t,H}</annotation></semantics></math> can take an infinite number of possible values, which would complicate our analysis, and also the implementation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to be able to predict the discretized return <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on any history <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math> and action <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. One can naturally consider augmenting the history with returns,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dots{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t-3}}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}z_{t-3}}{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}e_{t-3}}{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t-2}}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}z_{t-2}}{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}e_{t-2}}{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t-1}}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}z_{t-1}}{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}e_{t-1}}{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">a</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">a</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">a</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">a</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dots{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t-3}}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}z_{t-3}}{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}e_{t-3}}{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t-2}}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}z_{t-2}}{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}e_{t-2}}{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t-1}}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}z_{t-1}}{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}e_{t-1}}{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which would be passed in to a Bayesian sequence predictor to predict <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, a complication arises:
computing the ground truth return <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> requires the yet to be observed reward <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}e_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">e</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}e_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, the returns <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t-H+1},\dotsc,z_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t-H+1},\dotsc,z_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are all unavailable at time <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, instead of augmenting the sequence with returns at every step, we augment with a period <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq H</annotation></semantics></math>, at only the positions <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\equiv t\ (\mathrm{mod}\ N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.500em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\equiv t\ (\mathrm{mod}\ N)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dots{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t-2N}}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}z_{t-2N}}{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}e_{t-2N}}\dots{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t-N}}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}z_{t-N}}{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}e_{t-N}}\dots{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t-1}}{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}e_{t-1}}{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">a</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">−</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">N</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">−</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">N</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">a</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">N</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">z</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">N</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">a</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">e</mi><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">t</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">−</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#1E4678;" mathcolor="#1E4678">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">a</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#8C3232;" mathcolor="#8C3232">t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dots{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t-2N}}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}z_{t-2N}}{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}e_{t-2N}}\dots{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t-N}}{\color[rgb]{0,0,0}z_{t-N}}{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}e_{t-N}}\dots{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t-1}}{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}e_{t-1}}{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}a_{t}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Then, the augmented returns require rewards at times only up to <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-N+H-1\leq t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-N+H-1\leq t-1</annotation></semantics></math>, which are all available.
Why we don’t simply consider <math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=H</annotation></semantics></math> will be revealed in the proof of <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>.
The periodically augmented sequence can be passed in to a Bayesian sequence predictor, to predict the next return <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We formalize this below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Given an augmentation <em id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">period</em> <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq H</annotation></semantics></math>,
the augmented sample space associated with <em id="S3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">phase</em> <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{0,\dotsc,N-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{0,\dotsc,N-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex15X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex15X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\Omega}^{(n)}\hskip-2.0pt:=\prod_{i=1}^{\infty}\mathcal{B}^{(n)}_{i}\hskip-2.0pt,\hskip 7.2pt\mathcal{B}^{(n)}_{i}\hskip-2.0pt:=\hskip-2.0pt\begin{cases}\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{Z}\times\mathcal{E},&amp;\hskip-7.0pti\equiv n\ (\mathrm{mod}\ N),\\
\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{E},&amp;\hskip-7.0pt\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.887em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.500em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\Omega}^{(n)}\hskip-2.0pt:=\prod_{i=1}^{\infty}\mathcal{B}^{(n)}_{i}\hskip-2.0pt,\hskip 7.2pt\mathcal{B}^{(n)}_{i}\hskip-2.0pt:=\hskip-2.0pt\begin{cases}\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{Z}\times\mathcal{E},&amp;\hskip-7.0pti\equiv n\ (\mathrm{mod}\ N),\\
\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{E},&amp;\hskip-7.0pt\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">In other words, an augmented outcome <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}\in\tilde{\Omega}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}\in\tilde{\Omega}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}</annotation></semantics></math> contains some <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{z}_{i_{k}}\in\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{z}_{i_{k}}\in\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> at the periodic positions
<math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{k}=n+kN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{k}=n+kN</annotation></semantics></math>, in between <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that these augmented returns do not necessarily have to match the ground truth returns <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> computed with <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r_{i_{k}:i_{k}{+}H{-}1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i_{k}:i_{k}{+}H{-}1}</annotation></semantics></math>, in order for <math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}</annotation></semantics></math> to qualify as an element of <math id="S3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Omega}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Omega}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We call an augmented outcome <math id="S3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}</annotation></semantics></math> “valid” if its augmented returns match the ground truth returns.
The prefix <math id="S3.p4.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{h}_{1:t}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}_{1:t}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called an augmented history. The set of augmented histories is denoted <math id="S3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{H}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{H}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the set of augmented histories up to time <math id="S3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted <math id="S3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{H}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mtext class="ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{H}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">return-predictor</em> with phase <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is any mapping <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\bigcup_{i\equiv n}(\tilde{\mathcal{H}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}_{i-1}\times\mathcal{A})\to\Delta\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mtext class="ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\bigcup_{i\equiv n}(\tilde{\mathcal{H}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}_{i-1}\times\mathcal{A})\to\Delta\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. The domain consists exactly of all phase <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> augmented histories that end exactly at positions where the augmented return <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> should come next. We define the mixture return-predictor, like the mixture environment in <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2.2 (Mixture environment). ‣ 2.2 AIXI ‣ 2 Background ‣ A Model-Free Universal AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Mixture return-predictor)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a class <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of phase <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> return-predictors <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, and a prior distribution <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{n}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{n}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>, a mixture return-predictor <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique phase <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> return-predictor that satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{k=0}^{K}\psi_{n}(\tilde{z}_{i_{k}}\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i_{k}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i_{k}})\hskip-1.0pt=\hskip-2.0pt\sum_{\phi\in\mathcal{P}_{n}}\omega_{n}(\phi)\prod_{k=0}^{K}\phi(\tilde{z}_{i_{k}}\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i_{k}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i_{k}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{k=0}^{K}\psi_{n}(\tilde{z}_{i_{k}}\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i_{k}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i_{k}})\hskip-1.0pt=\hskip-2.0pt\sum_{\phi\in\mathcal{P}_{n}}\omega_{n}(\phi)\prod_{k=0}^{K}\phi(\tilde{z}_{i_{k}}\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i_{k}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i_{k}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{k}=n+kN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{k}=n+kN</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Equivalently, <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{n}(\tilde{z}_{i_{k}}\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i_{k}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mtext class="ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{n}(\tilde{z}_{i_{k}}\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i_{k}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the posterior predictive distribution</p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\phi\in\mathcal{P}_{n}}\omega_{n}(\phi\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i_{k-1}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i_{k-1}}\tilde{z}_{i_{k-1}})\,\phi(\tilde{z}_{i_{k}}\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i_{k}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i_{k}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mtext class="ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mtext class="ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\phi\in\mathcal{P}_{n}}\omega_{n}(\phi\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i_{k-1}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i_{k-1}}\tilde{z}_{i_{k-1}})\,\phi(\tilde{z}_{i_{k}}\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i_{k}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i_{k}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">with the posterior weights <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{n}(\phi\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i_{k}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i_{k}}\tilde{z}_{i_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mtext class="ltx_scalerel">(n)</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{n}(\phi\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i_{k}}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i_{k}}\tilde{z}_{i_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math> updated to</p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
