<p id="S3.p6.3" class="ltx_p">starting with <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{n}(\phi\mid\epsilon)=\omega_{n}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{n}(\phi\mid\epsilon)=\omega_{n}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the empty string.
In other words, by conditioning the mixture <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on a history, we are implicitly performing Bayesian inference over the return-predictors <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\aug_n : \Omega\hookrightarrow\tilde{\Omega}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}$betheinjectivemappingthataugmentsanoutcome$h$toitscorrespondingvalidaugmentedoutcome$\aug_n (h)$,byinsertingthetruereturns$z_{i_k}$computedwiththerewards$r_{i_k:i_k+H-1}$in$h$.Underacertaincondition,ahistory$h_{&lt;t}$uniquelydetermines$\aug_n(h)_{&lt;t}$regardlessof$h_{t:\infty}$,andinthatcaseweabusethenotation$\aug_n$toalsodenotethemapping$\aug_n(h_{&lt;t})=\aug_n(h)_{&lt;t}$.Fortunately,wecanstatethisconditionexplicitlyas:\begin{equation}(t-1-n)\,\mathrm{mod}\,N\geq H-1.\end{equation}Thisisbecausethelatestindex$i\equiv n \ (\mathrm{mod}\ N)$smallerthan$t$isexactly$t-1 - (t-1-n) \,\mathrm{mod}\,N$,andthereturnatthisindexrequiresrewardsattimesupto$t-1 - (t-1-n) \,\mathrm{mod}\,N + H -1 $,whichissmallerthan$t$ifandonlyif\lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{eq:condition_aug}holds.Inotherwords,alltherewardsrequiredforaugmentingwiththetruereturns$z_{i_k}$areavailablein$h_{&lt;t}$.Notethat\lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{eq:condition_aug}istriviallysatisfiedif$n=t \,\mathrm{mod}\,N$.Wecanhencedefinea\emph{unifiedpredictor},thatabstractsawaytheperiodicaugmentation.\begin{definition}Given a collection of $N$ mixture return-predictors $\{\psi_{n}\}_{0\leq n&lt;N}$, the unified predictor $\psi:\mathcal{H}\times\mathcal{A}\to\Delta\mathcal{Z}$ is given by
$$\psi(\tilde{z}_{t}\mid h_{&lt;t}a_{t}):=\psi_{n}(\tilde{z}_{t}\mid\aug_n (h_{&lt;t}) a_t)
$$
where $n=t\,\mathrm{mod}\,N$.
\end{definition}
\par Next, we define AIQI as follows. We also provide a pseudocode in \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{alg:aiqi}.
\begin{definition}Fix a return horizon $H$, a discretization level $M$, an augmentation period $N\geq H$, an exploration rate $\tau&gt;0$, and a unified predictor $\psi$.
AIQI, denoted $\hat{\pi}^{H,M,N,\tau}_{\psi}$ or $\hat{\pi}$, is defined as:
$$\hat{\pi}(a\mid h_{&lt;t}):=(1-\tau)\mathds{1}\left[a=a^{\ast}\right]+\tau/|\mathcal{A}|,$$where $a^{\ast}=\argmax_{a_{t}}\hat{Q}(h_{&lt;t},a_{t})$ and
$$\hat{Q}(h_{&lt;t},a_{t})=\sum_{\tilde{z}_{t}\in\mathcal{Z}}\tilde{z}_{t}\cdot\psi(\tilde{z}_{t}\mid h_{&lt;t}a_{t}).$$In case multiple actions maximize $\hat{Q}$, we assume a fixed tie-breaking rule.
\end{definition}
Note that AIQI doesn't simulate the environment or plan for the future.
Instead, AIQI directly predicts its own action-value, and at every step chooses the action that maximizes it.
As evidence accumulates, the value predictions become more and more accurate. In the limit, AIQI chooses the action that maximizes its true action-value, and will keep doing so in all future steps, thereby turning into a globally optimal policy.
Another way to look at AIQI is that it is performing policy evaluation at every step, treating the whole trajectory as having been generated by a single policy, and performing policy improvement in choosing the next action.
\par\begin{algorithm}\@@toccaption{{\lx@tag[ ]{{1}}{Universal AI with Q-Induction (AIQI)}}}\@@caption{{\lx@tag[ ]{{Algorithm 1}} {Universal AI with Q-Induction (AIQI)}}}\lx@algorithmicx@beginlist@{}
\lx@algorithmicx@@item Return horizon $H$, discretization level $M$, augmentation period $N\geq H$, exploration probability $\tau$, set of unified predictor $\psi$
\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 0.0ptHistory $h_{&lt;1}\leftarrow\epsilon$, time $t\leftarrow 1$
\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 0.0pt{loop}\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 15.33325pt{for}\ each action $a\in\mathcal{A}$\ {do}
\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 30.6665pt$\hat{Q}(h_{&lt;t},a)\leftarrow\sum_{\tilde{z}\in\mathcal{Z}}\tilde{z}\cdot\psi(\tilde{z}\mid h_{&lt;t},a)$
\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 15.33325pt{end}\ {for}\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 15.33325pt$a^{\ast}\leftarrow\operatorname{argmax}_{a\in\mathcal{A}}\hat{Q}(h_{&lt;t},a)$
\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 15.33325ptSample $p\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1]$
\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 15.33325pt{if}\ $p&lt;1-\tau$\ {then}
\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 30.6665pt$a_{t}\leftarrow a^{\ast}$ \lx@algorithmicx@hfill$\triangleright$ Exploit
\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 15.33325pt{else}\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 30.6665pt$a_{t}\sim\mathcal{U}(\mathcal{A})$ \lx@algorithmicx@hfill$\triangleright$ Explore
\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 15.33325pt{end}\ {if}\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 15.33325ptExecute $a_{t}$, observe $e_{t}$
\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 15.33325pt$h_{&lt;t+1}\leftarrow h_{&lt;t}a_{t}e_{t}$
\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 15.33325pt$t\leftarrow t+1$
\lx@algorithmicx@@item\noindent\hskip 0.0pt{end}\ {loop}\lx@algorithmicx@endlist
\end{algorithm}
\par As is common in the universal AI literature, AIQI faces the grain of truth (self-referential) problem \cite[citep]{[\@@bibref{AuthorsPhrase1Year}{kalai1993rational,leike2016formal,meulemans2025embedded}{\@@citephrase{, }}{}]}.
Roughly speaking, every mixture return-predictor $\psi_{n}$ in $\psi$ has to contain in its class the true conditional return distribution under the AIQI policy $\hat{\pi}_{\psi}$, which itself depends on $\psi$.
The formal statement is given below.
\begin{definition}Fix the AIQI parameters $H,M,N,\tau$.
A unified predictor $\psi$ has a grain of truth w.r.t. environment $\nu$ if: every $\psi_{n}$ is a mixture of a class $\mathcal{P}_{n}$ that contains the return-predictor
$\phi^{\ast}$ given by
$$\phi^{\ast}(\tilde{z}_{i}\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i})=\nu^{\hat{\pi}}(z_{i}=\tilde{z}_{i}\mid h_{&lt;i}a_{i}),\kern 5.0pti\equiv n\ (\mathrm{mod}\ N),$$where $\hat{\pi}=\hat{\pi}^{H,M,N,\tau}_{\psi}$ and the right hand side is the conditional return distribution under $\nu^{\hat{\pi}}$.
\end{definition}
A nontrivial solution can be obtained by fixing a reflective oracle $O$ \cite[citep]{[\@@bibref{AuthorsPhrase1Year}{fallenstein2015reflective,leike2016formal}{\@@citephrase{, }}{}]}, and setting $\mathcal{P}_{n}$ and $\mathcal{M}$ to be the class of all $O$-computable return-predictors and environments, respectively.
Under this setup, the AIQI policy $\hat{\pi}$ is $O$-computable, and for every $\nu\in\mathcal{M}$, $\phi^{\ast}=\nu^{\hat{\pi}}$ is also $O$-computable, hence inside $\mathcal{P}_{n}$.
\par We now introduce a notion of optimality that AIQI will be shown to satisfy. It is a more lenient form of strong asymptotic optimality, which is one of the strongest forms of optimality considered in the universal AI literature.
\begin{definition}Given $\varepsilon&gt;0$ and a class of environments $\mathcal{M}$, a policy $\pi$ is strong asymptotically $\varepsilon$-optimal if, for all environments $\nu\in\mathcal{M}$,
$$\limsup_{t\to\infty}V_{\nu}^{\ast}(h_{&lt;t})-V_{\nu}^{\pi}(h_{&lt;t})\leq\varepsilon,\quad\text{$\nu^{\pi}$-a.s.}$$\end{definition}
It has been shown by \cite[citet]{\@@bibref{Authors Phrase1YearPhrase2}{lattimore2011asymptotically}{\@@citephrase{[}}{\@@citephrase{, Theorem\penalty\ 8]}}} that any deterministic policy, including AIXI, is not strong asymptotically $\varepsilon$-optimal for all $\varepsilon&lt;1/4$.
\par\@@numbered@section{section}{toc}{Results}
\par The aim of this section is to prove that:
\begin{itemize}\itemize@item@For arbitrarily small $\varepsilon&gt;0$, one can choose parameters $H,M,N,\tau,\psi$ with which AIQI satisfies \emph{strong asymptotic $\varepsilon$-optimality}. (\lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{thm:aiqi-eps-optimal})
\par\vskip 4.0pt\vskip 4.0pt plus 2.0pt minus 1.0pt\itemize@item@With the same choice of parameters, AIQI is asymptotically $\varepsilon$-optimal in the mixture environment $\xi$. (\lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{thm:aiqi-approaches-aixi})
\end{itemize}
Notably, we do not invoke any novel, ad-hoc assumptions in proving our results.
The list of notations can be found in \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{sec:notation}, and omitted proofs are in \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{sec:proofs}.
We have also performed experiments with a computable approximation of AIQI, which we report in \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{sec:experimental}.
\par\par\@@numbered@section{subsection}{toc}{Convergence of Return-Predictor}
\par First, we show that the unified return-predictor $\psi$ converges to the true return-predictor $\phi^{\ast}=\nu^{\pi}$, when conditioned on increasingly longer histories generated by $\nu^{\pi}$.
A useful analytic device is the total variation (TV) distance.
Given two probability measures $P$ and $Q$ defined on a measurable space $(\Omega,\mathcal{F})$, the TV distance is defined as
$$D(P,Q):=\sup_{E\in\mathcal{F}}\,|P(E)-Q(E)|.$$
An elementary property of TV distance is that, for a countable set $\{E_{i}\}$ of pairwise disjoint events,
\begin{equation}\textstyle\sum_{i}|P(E_{i})-Q(E_{i})|\leq 2\cdot D(P,Q).\end{equation}
\par Given an environment $\nu$ and a policy $\pi$, define the phase $n$ augmented distribution $\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_{n}:=\nu^{\pi}\circ\aug_n^{-1} $asthepushforwardof$\nu^\pi$by$\aug_n$.Wecanexpress$\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_n$asaproductofconditionalprobabilitieswhosedependenciesrespectthewrittenorderin$\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}=
{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953} a_1}
{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688} e_1}
\ldots{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953} a_n}
\tilde{z}_n
{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688} e_n}
\ldots$,asfollows:\begin{aligned} &amp;\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_{n}(\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}})={\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}\prod_{i}\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_{n}(a_{i}\mid\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}_{&lt;i})}{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}\prod_{i\not\equiv{n}}\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_{n}(e_{i}\mid\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}_{&lt;i}a_{i})}\\
&amp;\hskip 45.0pt\cdot{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}\prod_{i\equiv{n}}\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_{n}(e_{i}\mid\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}_{&lt;i}a_{i}\tilde{z}_{i})}\prod_{i\equiv n}\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_{n}(\tilde{z}_{i}\mid\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}_{&lt;i}a_{i}).\end{aligned}Notethattheabovecharacterizationof$\tilde{\nu}^\pi_n$isinformal,as$\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_n (\tilde{h} ^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}})$isjust0formost$\tilde{h} ^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}$.Apropercharacterizationwouldinvolve$\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_n (\tilde{h} ^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}_{&lt;t})$andallproductsindexedupto$i=t-1$.Weusetheinformalnotationforbrevity.\par Thelastfactorin\lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{eq:conditional-tilde-nu}issimply$\nu^{\pi}(z_i=\tilde{z}_i \mid{h}_{&lt;i} a_i)$,whichweabbreviateas$
\nu^{\pi}(\tilde{z}_i \mid{h}_{&lt;i} a_i)
$.Letusalsoabbreviatethefirstthreefactorsin\lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{eq:conditional-tilde-nu}as$\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_n (h \parallel\tilde{z}_{i\equiv n})$,sothat\begin{equation}\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_{n}(\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}})=\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_{n}(h\parallel\tilde{z}_{i\equiv n})\prod_{i\equiv n}\nu^{\pi}(\tilde{z}_{i}\mid{h}_{&lt;i}a_{i}).\end{equation}\par Let$\pi$betheAIQIpolicy$\hat{\pi}$withsomeparameters$H$,$M$,$N$,$\tau$,$\psi$.Supposeitsmixturereturn-predictor$\psi_n$in$\psi$isamixtureofreturn-predictorsincluding$\phi^\ast$givenby$$\phi^{\ast}(\tilde{z}_{i}\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i})=\nu^{\pi}(\tilde{z}_{i}\mid h_{&lt;i}a_{i}),\quad i\equiv n\ (\mathrm{mod}\ N),$$whichisexactlythegrainoftruthcondition.Then,define$$q_{n}(\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}):=\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_{n}(h\parallel\tilde{z}_{i\equiv n})\prod_{i\equiv n}\psi_{n}(\tilde{z}_{i}\mid\tilde{h}_{&lt;i}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}a_{i}),$$whichis\lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{eq:tilde-nu-def}with$\nu^\pi=\phi^\ast$replacedby$\psi_n$.Intuitively,wewanttoshowthatourmixture$\psi_n$convergesto$\phi^\ast$,andwedosobyfirstshowingthat$q_n$convergesto$\tilde{\nu}^\pi_n$.\par Bythedefinitionofamixturein\lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{def:mixture-return-predictor},\begin{aligned} q_{n}(\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}})&amp;\geq\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_{n}(h\parallel\tilde{z}_{i\equiv n})\left(\omega_{n}(\phi^{\ast})\prod_{i\equiv n}\phi^{\ast}(\tilde{z}_{i}\mid\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}_{&lt;i}a_{i})\right)\\
&amp;=\omega_{n}(\phi^{\ast})\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_{n}(\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}).\end{aligned}Bytheinequality,$\tilde{\nu}^\pi_n$isabsolutelycontinuouswithrespectto$q_n$,sothatby\cite[citet]{\@@bibref{Authors Phrase1YearPhrase2}{blackwell1962merging}{\@@citephrase{[}}{\@@citephrase{]}}},$$\lim_{t\to\infty}D(q_{n},\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_{n}\mid\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}_{&lt;t})=0,\quad\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_{n}\text{-a.s.}$$\par Wecanthenprovethefollowinglemma.Intuitively,$\tilde{\nu}^\pi_n$isthepushforwardof$\nu^\pi$by$\aug_n$,sowecanreplace$\tilde{h}^{\lx@scalerel@obj{(n)}}_{&lt;t}$with${{\aug_n}(h)}_{&lt;t}$,and$\tilde{\nu}^\pi_n$-with$\nu^\pi$-almostsureconvergence.\begin{lemma}Let $\pi$ be the AIQI policy $\hat{\pi}^{H,M,N,\tau}_{\psi}$ where $\psi$ has a grain of truth w.r.t. an environment $\nu$.
There exists a $\nu^{\pi}$-probability-one set $S\subseteq\Omega$ such that for all $h\in S$ and $n\in[0,N-1]$,
$$\lim_{t\to\infty}D(q_{n},\tilde{\nu}^{\pi}_{n}\mid{{\aug_n}(h)}_{&lt;t}) = 0.
$$
\end{lemma}
\par\par We are now ready to show that each mixture return-predictor $\psi_{n}$, or more generally the unified predictor $\psi$, converges to the true return-predictor $\phi^{\ast}=\nu^{\pi}$.
By ``convergence'', one would usually mean something along the line of
\begin{equation}\lim_{t\to\infty}\sum_{\tilde{z}_{t}\in\mathcal{Z}}\left|\psi(\tilde{z}_{t}|h_{&lt;t}\,a_{t})-\nu^{\pi}(\tilde{z}_{t}|h_{&lt;t}\,a_{t})\right|=0.\end{equation}
Instead, we prove something strictly stronger, involving a sum over counterfactual trajectories $h^{\prime}_{t:m-1}$ that extend $h_{&lt;t}$.
This stronger form of convergence is essential for proving \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{thm:aiqi-eps-optimal} without invoking any ad-hoc assumptions, e.g., off-policy sensibility \cite[citep]{[\@@bibref{AuthorsPhrase1Year}{catt2023self}{\@@citephrase{, }}{}, Theorem\penalty\ 16]}.
\par Let us denote the return-predictor error by
$$\delta_{\psi}(h_{&lt;t}a_{t}):=\sum_{\tilde{z}_{t}\in\mathcal{Z}}\left|\psi(\tilde{z}_{t}\mid h_{&lt;t}\,a_{t})-\nu^{\pi}(\tilde{z}_{t}\mid h_{&lt;t}\,a_{t})\right|,$$
and let $\mathcal{T}$ be the set of counterfactual trajectories $h^{\prime}_{&lt;m}$,
\@@amsalign
 formed by $h_{&lt;t}$ appended with all possible percept sequences of length $m-t$ and reactions from some deterministic policy $\pi^{\prime}$.
Then we can show the following.
\begin{lemma}Let $\pi$ be the AIQI policy $\hat{\pi}^{H,M,N,\tau}_{\psi}$ where $\psi$ has a grain of truth w.r.t. an environment $\nu$.
For every $\beta&gt;0$, there exists a $\nu^{\pi}$-probability-one set $S\subseteq\Omega$ where, for every outcome $h\in S$, there exists $t_{0}$ such that: for $t\geq t_{0}$ and $m\in[t,t{+}N{-}H]$,
\@@amsalign
\end{lemma}
A corollary of our result is \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{eq:return-predictor-error-usual}, obtained by setting $m=t$.
Remarkably, our result is stronger, bounding the sum over counterfactual trajectories for all $m\in[t,t+N-H]$.
This is possible due to the subtle fact that ${\aug_n}(h_{&lt;t})$iswell-definedfor$n=m \,\mathrm{mod}\,N$,accordingto\lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{eq:condition_aug}.Thissubtlefactalsorevealswhywecan^{\prime}tsimplyset$N=H$andhavetoaddabufferoflength$N-H$.Infact,thelengthofthisbufferisexactlythemaximumlengthofcounterfactualtrajectoriesthatcanextend$h_{&lt;t}$inourbound.\par\par\par\@@numbered@section{subsection}{toc}{One-Step Optimality}
\par We continue to denote the AIQI policy $\hat{\pi}$ as $\pi$.
Let the AIQI parameter $H$ be the $\eta$-effective horizon $H(\eta)$ for some $\eta&gt;0$.
We can show that the estimated action-value $\hat{Q}$ in \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{def:aiqi} converges to the true action-value $Q^{\pi}_{\nu}$.
Denote the Q-value estimation error by
$$\delta_{Q}(h_{&lt;t}a_{t}):=|\hat{Q}(h_{&lt;t}a_{t})-Q_{\nu}^{\pi}(h_{&lt;t}a_{t})|.$$
\begin{lemma}Under the conditions established in \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{thm:error-return-predictor},
\@@amsalign
\end{lemma}
\par Next, we show that the difference between the on-policy value and maximum Q-value becomes small.
We call this difference the one-step optimality gap,
$$\delta_{1}(h_{&lt;t}):=\max_{a}Q^{\pi}_{\nu}(h_{&lt;t},a)-V^{\pi}_{\nu}(h_{&lt;t}),$$
which is non-negative due to $V(\cdot)=\mathbb{E}_{a}[Q(\cdot,a)]$.
\begin{lemma}Under the conditions established in \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{thm:error-return-predictor},
\@@amsalign
\end{lemma}
Intuitively, since AIQI chooses the action $a^{\ast}$ that maximizes the estimate $\hat{Q}$, the value $V$ of AIQI is approximately equal to its Q-value under the action $a^{\ast}$, with only a slight difference due to the exploration probability $\tau$.
As the estimate $\hat{Q}$ converges to the true Q-value due to \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{thm:error-q-estimation}, the Q-value $Q(\cdot,a^{\ast})$, and hence the value $V(\cdot)$, converges to the true maximum $\max_{a}Q(\cdot,a)$.
\par\par\@@numbered@section{subsection}{toc}{Asymptotic Optimality}
Let the global optimality gap be
$$\delta_{\infty}(h_{&lt;t}):=V^{\ast}_{\nu}(h_{&lt;t})-V^{\pi}_{\nu}(h_{&lt;t}).$$
We now want to show the convergence of the global optimality gap, which is equivalent to asymptotic optimality.
First, we present a lemma that relates the global optimality gap to the one-step optimality gap.
\begin{lemma}For any environment $\nu$, policy $\pi$, and history $h_{&lt;t}$,
$$\delta_{\infty}(h_{&lt;t})\leq\gamma\sum_{e^{\prime}_{t}}\nu(e^{\prime}_{t}\mid h_{&lt;t}\,a^{\prime}_{t})\,\delta_{\infty}(h_{&lt;t}\,a^{\prime}_{t}\,e^{\prime}_{t})+\delta_{1}(h_{&lt;t}),$$where
$a^{\prime}_{t}=\argmax_{a}\left(Q^{\ast}_{\nu}(h_{&lt;t},a)-Q^{\pi}_{\nu}(h_{&lt;t},a)\right)$.
\end{lemma}
\par Note that we can form a \emph{chain} of inequalities by repeatedly applying the lemma to $\delta_{\infty}$ on the right hand side, as follows:
\@@amsalign
 where
$a^{\prime}_{i}=\argmax_{a}|Q^{\ast}_{\nu}(h^{\prime}_{&lt;i},a)-Q^{\pi}_{\nu}(h^{\prime}_{&lt;i},a)|$.
\par Furthermore, the first term in the last inequality is
smaller than or equal to $\gamma^{L}$, since $\delta_{\infty}(h^{\prime}_{&lt;t+L})\leq 1$.
Thus $\delta_{\infty}(h_{&lt;t})$ is smaller than or equal to
\begin{equation}\gamma^{L}\!+\!\sum_{l=0}^{L-1}\gamma^{l}\hskip-4.0pt\sum_{e^{\prime}_{t:t+l-1}}\hskip-6.0pt\nu(e^{\prime}_{t:t+l-1}|h_{&lt;t}\|a^{\prime}_{t:t+l-1})\,\delta_{1}(h^{\prime}_{&lt;t+l}).\end{equation}
We have bound the global optimality gap $\delta_{\infty}(h_{&lt;t})$ using \emph{only} one-step optimality gaps $\delta_{1}$, albeit on counterfactual trajectories.
\par We are now ready to prove the central result of our paper, that AIQI is strong asymptotically $\varepsilon$-optimal.
\begin{theorem}Fix an environment class $\mathcal{M}$ and tolerance $\varepsilon&gt;0$.
Let
$\tau\leq\frac{\varepsilon(1-\gamma)}{10}$,
$M\geq\frac{10}{\varepsilon(1-\gamma)}$,
$H=H(\eta)$ with $\eta\leq\frac{\varepsilon(1-\gamma)}{10}$, and
$N\geq H+\log_{\gamma}\frac{\varepsilon}{5}$.
Suppose $\psi$ is a unified predictor that has a grain of truth with respect to all environments $\nu\in\mathcal{M}$ and the above parameters for AIQI.
Then, the AIQI policy $\hat{\pi}^{H,M,N,\tau}_{\psi}$ is strong asymptotically $\varepsilon$-optimal in $\mathcal{M}$.
\end{theorem}
\@proof We simply write $\pi$ for the AIQI policy $\hat{\pi}^{H,M,N,\tau}_{\psi}$.
Fix an environment $\nu\in\mathcal{M}$, and let $L=N-H+1$.
Fix some $\beta&gt;0$, and with it, choose $S\subseteq\Omega$ as in \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{thm:error-return-predictor}. For any $h\in S$, choose $t_{0}$, again as in \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{thm:error-return-predictor}. Then for all $t\geq t_{0}$, substituting $m=t+l$ in \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{eq:chain-global-one-step} and noting that $a^{\prime}_{i}$ is the output of a deterministic policy, we apply \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{thm:error-one-step} to obtain
\@@amsalign
 Recall that
$L\geq\log_{\gamma}\frac{\varepsilon}{5}$,
$\tau\leq\frac{\varepsilon(1-\gamma)}{10}$,
$M\geq\frac{10}{\varepsilon(1-\gamma)}$,
and
$\eta\leq\frac{\varepsilon(1-\gamma)}{10}$.
If we use
$\beta=\frac{\varepsilon\tau}{20}|\mathcal{A}|^{-2}(\sum_{l=0}^{L-1}(\gamma|\mathcal{A}|/\tau)^{l})^{-1}$ in choosing $S$ and $t_{0}$,
the right hand side is smaller than or equal to $\varepsilon$.
Thus for $h\in S$ and $t\geq t_{0}$, $\delta_{\infty}(h_{&lt;t})\leq\varepsilon$.
This concludes our proof that
$$\limsup_{t\to\infty}\delta_{\infty}(h_{&lt;t})\leq\varepsilon,\quad\text{$\nu^{\pi}$-a.s.}\qed$$
\end@proof
\par We can also show that strong asymptotic $\varepsilon$-optimality in the environment class $\mathcal{M}$ implies asymptotic optimality in the mixture environment $\xi$.
\begin{lemma}Given a mixture $\xi$ of environment class $\mathcal{M}$,
and a policy $\pi$ that is strong asymptotically $\varepsilon$-optimal with respect to $\mathcal{M}$,
the same policy $\pi$ is asymptotically $\varepsilon$-optimal in $\xi$:
$$\limsup_{t\to\infty}V_{\xi}^{\ast}(h_{&lt;t})-V_{\xi}^{\pi}(h_{&lt;t})\leq\varepsilon$$holds both $\xi^{\pi}$-a.s. and, for all $\nu\in\mathcal{M}$, $\nu^{\pi}$-a.s.
\end{lemma}
\par As a consequence, AIQI is also asymptotically optimal in $\xi$.
In other words, AIQI approximates \emph{Bayes-optimality}, which is the defining feature of AIXI.
\begin{theorem}For any mixture $\xi$ of environment class $\mathcal{M}$,
the AIQI policy $\hat{\pi}^{H,M,N,\tau}_{\psi}$ with the parameters in \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{thm:aiqi-eps-optimal} is asymptotically $\varepsilon$-optimal in $\xi$.
\end{theorem}
\@proof\lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{thm:aiqi-eps-optimal} and \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{thm:strong-implies-bayes}.
\qed\end@proof
\par\par\@@numbered@section{subsection}{toc}{Off-Policy Behavior}
\par Until now we have solely discussed the on-policy setting, where the policy that interacts with the environment is the same as the policy that we are optimizing.
In the \emph{off-policy setting}, we must infer an optimal policy from the interactions generated by some ``historic policy'' $\pi^{\prime}$ that
we {do not} have control over.
We can formalize this notion of optimality with the \emph{self-optimizing} property.
\begin{definition}A policy $\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}\bar{\pi}$ is called self-optimizing for a class of environments $\mathcal{M}$ and a historic policy $\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}\pi^{\prime}$, if for every $\nu\in\mathcal{M}$,
$$\lim_{t\to\infty}V_{\nu}^{\ast}(h_{&lt;t})-V_{\nu}^{{\color[rgb]{0.5508,0.1953,0.1953}\bar{\pi}}}(h_{&lt;t})=0,\quad\nu^{\color[rgb]{0.1172,0.2734,0.4688}\pi^{\prime}}\text{-a.s.}$$\end{definition}
\cite[citet]{\@@bibref{Authors Phrase1YearPhrase2}{hutter2002self}{\@@citephrase{[}}{\@@citephrase{]}}} showed a remarkable property of AIXI, that if there \emph{exists} a self-optimizing policy for $\mathcal{M}$ and $\pi^{\prime}$, then AIXI is one of those self-optimizing policies.
On the other hand, we can expect that AIQI does not satisfy this property, since it is an on-policy MC control algorithm.
\par In fact, if the predictor $\psi$ supports the return-predictor $\phi^{\ast}=\nu^{\pi^{\prime}}$, AIQI will eventually choose actions with the highest value under the historic policy $\pi^{\prime}$, and not its own value---the optimality of AIQI is thus no longer guaranteed. Even if we relax the self-optimizing property to the $\varepsilon$-self-optimizing property by replacing $\lim=0$ with $\limsup\leq\varepsilon$, the following can be shown.
\begin{theorem}Suppose the unified predictor $\psi$ is built from classes $\mathcal{P}_{n}$ that contain all computable variable-order Markov models.
For any period $N$, exploration rate $\tau$, horizon $H\geq 2$, tolerance $\varepsilon$ small enough (depending on $H$), and discretization level $M$ large enough
(depending on $H,\varepsilon,\tau$), there exist
an environment class $\mathcal{M}$, a historic policy $\pi^{\prime}$, and a discount factor
$\gamma\in(0,1)$, for which there exists a self-optimizing policy, but the AIQI policy $\hat{\pi}^{H,M,N,\tau}_{\psi}$ is not even $\varepsilon$-self-optimizing.
\end{theorem}
\par This completes our investigation of the theoretical properties of AIQI.
We have assumed a geometric discount setting for simplicity,
but we can extend all our results to the general discount setting, where returns are defined as $R_{t}=\sum_{k=t}^{\infty}\gamma_{k}r_{k}/\sum_{k=t}^{\infty}\gamma_{k}$ for a general discount sequence $\gamma_{k}$.
Specifically, our results can be generalized to cases where the discount sequence decays faster than a geometric sequence.
We detail the generalization in \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{sec:arbitrary_discount}.
\par
\par\@@numbered@section{section}{toc}{Related Work}
