                                                                                6.1
                                                                                §6.1
                                                                              
                                                                              6.1Proof Technique
                                                                              
                                                                                To prove that AIQI is strong asymptotically 
                                                                                    
                                                                                      ε
                                                                                    
                                                                                  -optimal, we first showed that the action-value estimates converge to the true 
                                                                                    
                                                                                      
                                                                                        
                                                                                        ν
                                                                                        π
                                                                                      
                                                                                    
                                                                                   action-values (), and that choosing the action with the highest (estimated) 
                                                                                    
                                                                                      
                                                                                        
                                                                                        ν
                                                                                        π
                                                                                      
                                                                                    
                                                                                   value makes the policy converge to optimal actions ().
There are two key novelties in our proofs compared to existing work in universal AI.
First, we had to deal with the problem of delayed feedback using periodic augmentations. This was not needed in previous works which model policies or environments, since actions and percepts are never delayed, unlike 
                                                                                    
                                                                                      H
                                                                                    
                                                                                  -step returns.
Second, we introduced a chain of inequalities using , to make the 
                                                                                    
                                                                                      
                                                                                        
                                                                                        δ
                                                                                        ∞
                                                                                      
                                                                                    
                                                                                   term in the upper bound vanish with 
                                                                                    
                                                                                      
                                                                                        
                                                                                        γ
                                                                                        L
                                                                                      
                                                                                    
                                                                                  .
This allowed us to bypass ad-hoc assumptions such as off-policy sensibility
[Catt et al., 2023, Theorem 16].
Overall, our work provides a blueprint for the analysis of policy iteration algorithms in general environments.
                                                                              
                                                                            
                                                                            
                                                                              
                                                                                6.2
                                                                                subsection 6.2
                                                                                §
                                                                                Section
                                                                                §§
                                                                                Sections
                                                                                6.2
                                                                                §6.2
                                                                              
                                                                              6.2Partial Observability
                                                                              
                                                                                Real-world RL tasks are almost never plain MDPs, due to partial observability [aastrom1965optimal].
The most straightforward way to extend MDP methods to partially observable MDPs (POMDPs) is by viewing the whole history as a state, and using function approximators, sequence models in particular, to handle this exponentially larger state space.
In fact, this is exactly the dominant approach in modern RL, where sequence models such as LSTMs [hochreiter1997long] or transformers [vaswani2017attention] are trained to model value functions [hausknecht2015deep], policies [mnih2016asynchronous], or environments [ha2018world].
                                                                              
                                                                              
                                                                                The theory of universal AI provides a particularly compelling account that is in line with practice: it regards the sequence model as a flexible learner with some broad prior over mappings whose domain consists of arbitrary length sequences.
In particular, it abstracts away the idea of a hidden MDP or a hidden state space.
Our work is the first to analyze a model-free algorithm under this account.
                                                                              
                                                                            
                                                                            
                                                                              
                                                                                6.3
                                                                                subsection 6.3
                                                                                §
                                                                                Section
                                                                                §§
                                                                                Sections
                                                                                6.3
                                                                                §6.3
                                                                              
                                                                              6.3Continual Reinforcement Learning
                                                                              
                                                                                The class of general environments is equivalent to the class of infinite-state POMDPs, which might seem unnecessarily large.
However, there are nontrivial settings that cannot be captured by finite-state POMDPs, one such example being continual RL [ring1994continual].
Continual RL studies environments that never stop changing [khetarpal2022towards], and where agents must keep learning in order to act optimally [abel2023definition].
The theory of universal AI sets aside problems with continual learning, e.g., catastrophic forgetting, by assuming a perfect Bayesian learner with a broad prior.
This lets it focus on a different question:
how to design principled objectives for continual RL, assuming continual learning is approximately solved.
Our work shows that learning distributional action-values is one such objective.
                                                                              
                                                                            
                                                                            
                                                                              
                                                                                6.4
                                                                                subsection 6.4
                                                                                §
                                                                                Section
                                                                                §§
                                                                                Sections
                                                                                6.4
                                                                                §6.4
                                                                              
                                                                              6.4Temporal Difference Learning
                                                                              
                                                                                Most practical methods in model-free RL involve temporal difference (TD) learning [sutton1988learning],
a form of stochastic approximation [robbins1951stochastic].
As has been noted by engel2003bayes, engel2005reinforcement, a simple Bayesian version of TD learning does not model the correct generative process of a value function, except in deterministic environments.
A possible workaround is to perform full expectation updates by learning an environment model.
This is analogous to Dyna [sutton1990integrated] with one-step rollouts.
We hypothesize that distributional, one-step Dyna-SARSA and Dyna-Q are, respectively, asymptotically optimal and self-optimizing.
                                                                              
                                                                              
                                                                                
                                                                                  7
                                                                                  section 7
                                                                                  §
                                                                                  Section
                                                                                  §§
                                                                                  Sections
                                                                                  7
                                                                                  §7
                                                                                
                                                                                7Conclusion
                                                                                
                                                                                  We introduced Universal AI with Q-Induction (AIQI), the first model-free universal agent. AIQI performs universal induction over distributional action-value functions—rather than over environments or policies as in prior work.
It is essentially a Monte Carlo control algorithm, the most primitive form of model-free RL.
We prove that AIQI is strong asymptotically 
                                                                                      
                                                                                        ε
                                                                                      
                                                                                    -optimal, asymptotically 
                                                                                      
                                                                                        ε
                                                                                      
                                                                                    -Bayes-optimal, and, as expected from an on-policy MC control algorithm, not self-optimizing.
Remarkably, the only assumption we make is grain of truth, which is standard in universal AI and has a known resolution via reflective oracles.
                                                                                
                                                                                
                                                                                  Our findings significantly broaden the known landscape of universal agents.
We hope this provides both a theoretical foundation for understanding MC control with sequence models in partially observable or continual settings,
and a blueprint for the analysis of other policy iteration algorithms in general environments.
Promising directions for future work include
exploring whether a single unified predictor can replace the phase-indexed family of return-predictors,
investigating whether a Bayesian version of temporal difference learning can be used to construct asymptotically optimal or self-optimizing policies,
and analyzing the properties of AIQI with exploration strategies that are more sophisticated than 
                                                                                      
                                                                                        ε
                                                                                      
                                                                                    -greedy, such as Thompson sampling.
                                                                                
                                                                                
